00:06:44,460 --> 00:06:48,460
So what are kind of the ideal conditions in a troop

137
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:50,760
for continual leadership development.

138
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:53,860
I say the single greatest key to strong youth

139
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:56,860
development is the attitude and actions of adults

140
00:06:56,860 --> 00:06:57,960
associated with the troop.

141
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:00,360
If they're not invested in creating the conditions

142
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:02,760
required for continuing youth leader development,

143
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:06,060
things will progress very slowly if at all.

144
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:08,560
There's a basic paradox to face here.

145
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:11,360
Youth leader development in its earliest stages

146
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:13,560
will not look like development at all.

147
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,560
In fact it will look like things are moving

148
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:16,160
backwards.

149
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:21,260
Getting past that stage requires an exceptional

150
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:25,060
quality in adults and that exceptional quality

151
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:28,860
I borrow from the National Outdoor Leadership School

152
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:30,960
in a leadership quality

153
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:34,960
They identify as capacity for adversity and uncertainty.

154
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:36,460
I'm going to say that again:

155
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:41,460
Capacity for adversity and uncertainty. scouts

156
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:43,460
developing as leaders won't perform at the same

157
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:45,960
level as adults. in the first stages of development

158
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:49,960
Their performance may even be hard to detect, let

159
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:53,260
alone evaluate. when things go undone or mistakes

160
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:55,159
are made, adults tend to step in

161
00:07:55,659 --> 00:07:59,659
And, as we noted in our first installment, adults

162
00:07:59,659 --> 00:08:01,760
can grow so discouraged with this whole process

163
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:03,960
They give up the idea on developing scouts into

164
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:06,760
leaders in any real, substantive way.

165
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:11,260
Scouts in their families also need some capacity

166
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:14,560
for uncertainty. as we are developing youth leaders,

167
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:18,159
Families especially need an initial introduction

168
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:20,860
and continual reinforcement that youth leaders

169
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:23,360
are at the helm and that they are developing

170
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:26,160
leaders, not seasoned professionals.

171
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,760
Many families initial experience scouting through

172
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:31,360
a cub pack and they've probably developed really

173
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:34,760
high expectations for order and communication

174
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:39,560
and consistency. and the chaos, incomplete communication

175
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:42,060
and the inconsistency of developing youth leaders

176
00:08:42,060 --> 00:08:43,960
can be a real big challenge for them.

177
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:46,660
It's right for the adult volunteers in a troop

178
00:08:46,660 --> 00:08:49,960
to communicate directly with parents through some

179
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:52,760
kind of parallel channel as the youth leaders

180
00:08:52,760 --> 00:08:54,060
communicate with scouts.

181
00:08:54,460 --> 00:08:57,160
They need to balance the effort to create a capacity

182
00:08:57,160 --> 00:08:59,160
in their youth leaders to communicate detail

183
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:02,160
plans with effective communication to families.

184
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:03,760
These are two different things.

185
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:07,360
Scouts are going to be contacting and talking

186
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:10,260
to scouts and the adult volunteers in the troop

187
00:09:10,460 --> 00:09:13,160
are going to back this up with this kind of

188
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:17,060
parallel communication with scouts families.

189
00:09:17,060 --> 00:09:21,560
We don't necessarily have to repeat everything

190
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:22,260
that is said.

191
00:09:22,260 --> 00:09:24,960
We don't necessarily have to add to the things

192
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:27,760
that were said, but we need to let our scouting

193
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:30,560
families know that we're right there when they

194
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:34,060
have questions, that we know what's going on

195
00:09:34,460 --> 00:09:37,660
and that we will help them get answers and will help

196
00:09:38,060 --> 00:09:41,160
kind of level out that inconsistency of communication

197
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:42,960
that comes from youth leaders very often

198
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:46,060
as they're developing in some ways. adult

199
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:49,060
volunteers are going to serve as a buffer

200
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:51,360
between families and youth leaders.

201
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:55,260
So, as a buffer, what we do is protect the

202
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:58,260
developmental environment. for youth leaders to

203
00:09:58,260 --> 00:10:00,860
develop, they have to have this safe environment

204
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:03,160
that's free from a lot of coercion, a lot of

205
00:10:03,160 --> 00:10:07,060
pressure to to begin to learn and develop

206
00:10:07,060 --> 00:10:10,260
as as leaders. and I would liken it to a vegetable

207
00:10:10,260 --> 00:10:10,660
garden.

208
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:13,060
You know your vegetable garden needs protection

209
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:15,460
from pests, just as developing youth leaders

210
00:10:15,460 --> 00:10:18,160
need protection from outside influences

211
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:20,560
that are going to disrupt the process. as they

212
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:22,860
develop, they're going to become more confident

213
00:10:22,860 --> 00:10:24,960
in addressing the concerns of adults and they

214
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:26,660
will begin to stand up for themselves.

215
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:30,160
But until they have this confidence, they need

216
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:32,760
to be protected, just like a little seedling

217
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:35,260
in the garden needs to be protected. we've got

218
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:37,760
to look out for the scouts. many things that

219
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:40,460
are detrimental to this developmental environment

220
00:10:40,460 --> 00:10:42,660
are minimized if we maintain balanced

221
00:10:42,660 --> 00:10:45,360
relationships between youth leaders and

222
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:47,760
their adult counterparts. now that balanced

223
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:52,060
relationship is not necessarily constant

224
00:10:52,260 --> 00:10:54,260
oversight, advice and over instruction,

225
00:10:54,260 --> 00:10:57,060
because that tends to stifle the discovery

226
00:10:57,060 --> 00:10:59,860
crucial to leadership development. adults

227
00:10:59,860 --> 00:11:01,660
working with youth leaders need to use

228
00:11:01,660 --> 00:11:05,060
a minimal amount of telling, communication

229
00:11:05,459 --> 00:11:07,360
or direct instructions.

230
00:11:07,760 --> 00:11:09,360
They should also understand that youth

231
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:11,459
leaders have different levels of tolerance

232
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:14,160
for mentoring and advising. too little

233
00:11:14,560 --> 00:11:17,160
mentoring and advising and oversight and

234
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:20,360
advice is almost always better than too

235
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:24,860
much. our role is more reflective than

236
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:27,760
directive. there's an important difference

237
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:31,760
there. our role is more reflective than

238
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:35,660
directive, where we are available but not

239
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:38,660
intrusive. when we balance this relationship,

240
00:11:38,660 --> 00:11:40,860
we create a safe environment for

241
00:11:41,160 --> 00:11:43,860
experiential development. now, given a goal

242
00:11:43,860 --> 00:11:46,360
or a task, youth leaders will seek a solution.

243
00:11:46,860 --> 00:11:49,060
if their proposed approach is not dangerous

244
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:50,459
or inappropriate, they should be allowed

245
00:11:50,459 --> 00:11:52,160
to make it happen, and this is important,

246
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:55,360
even if we're confident that that approach

247
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:59,160
may not work. the process of trial and

248
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:02,160
error leads to discovery. and think

249
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:05,560
about Edison. Edison tried out a lot of

250
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:07,860
materials before he discovered which

251
00:12:07,860 --> 00:12:10,760
worked well for a light bulb filament.

252
00:12:10,760 --> 00:12:14,260
he developed the light bulb experientially.

253
00:12:15,760 --> 00:12:17,460
now, the difference between Edison and our

254
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:19,160
developing youth leaders is: Edison was

255
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:21,060
looking for an unknown. our youth leaders

256
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:23,260
are discovering something that we probably

257
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:25,860
already know how to do. if we tell them

258
00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:28,460
everything we know, we deny them the more

259
00:12:28,460 --> 00:12:30,460
powerful and more lasting experience of

260
00:12:30,460 --> 00:12:32,260
discovering it for themselves. if we

261
00:12:32,260 --> 00:12:35,260
tell them what we know, if we say, hey,

262
00:12:35,260 --> 00:12:36,860
you know what I have the answer to this,

